---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

/**
 * The three steps are deliberately specific — a two-hour drive cap, per-person
 * budgets, entry rules dated on the page. Vague promises are what every other
 * travel site makes; these are checkable.
 */
const method = [
  {
    step: 'Build the days around the walking, not the map',
    body: `Drives get capped at roughly two hours. Big sights land before lunch,
      when the queues are short and the light is good. Nothing on the plan
      assumes you will happily queue for an hour after a long flight, because
      nobody does.`,
  },
  {
    step: 'Price it in real numbers',
    body: `Budgets are per person, based on mid-range hotels and the tours
      actually linked on the page — not a range wide enough to be useless.
      Every figure converts live, so it is right on the day you read it.`,
  },
  {
    step: 'Say where to check the entry rules',
    body: `What you need to enter depends on your passport, so a plan that states
      one answer would be wrong for most readers. Each itinerary points at the
      authority that can actually answer it, and says when it was last checked.`,
  },
];

/** Countries I have travelled myself, as opposed to researched. */
const visited = ['India', 'Japan', 'Russia', 'Georgia', 'Armenia', 'the UAE'];
---
<Base
  title="About — The Itinerary Wala"
  description="Written by Nitesh Parasher, a software engineer and Himalayan trekker. Why these itineraries exist, how they get built, and where I have actually been."
  image="/images/nitesh-himalaya.jpeg"
  imageAlt="Nitesh Parasher on a Himalayan pass"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>About</p>
      <h1 data-reveal>Plans built for how people actually travel.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__lede" data-reveal>
        Most itineraries online read like a list of sights. They rarely say
        how long the drives are, what the week actually costs, or which of them
        is worth the queue and which one isn't.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="author" data-reveal>
      <figure class="author__shot">
        <img
          src="/images/nitesh-himalaya.jpeg"
          alt="Nitesh Parasher holding an Indian flag on a Himalayan pass, snow peaks behind"
          width="675"
          height="900"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>On the pass, Indian Himalaya</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="author__body prose">
        <h2>Who writes these</h2>
        <p>
          I'm <strong>Nitesh Parasher</strong> — a software engineer who plans
          other people's holidays for fun.
        </p>
        <p>
          The travelling started in the Himalayas. Bali Pass, Pir Panjal, Rupin
          Pass, Kedarkantha, Pin Bhaba — the kind of trekking where the day's
          plan is the difference between a good walk and a miserable one. You
          learn fast that <em>"it's about four hours"</em> in someone else's
          trip report is doing a great deal of work.
        </p>
        <p>
          That habit followed me off the mountain. I've travelled nearly every
          state and union territory in India, and worked my way through Japan,
          Russia, Georgia, Armenia and the UAE. Every time, the same thing:
          itineraries written to sound good rather than to be followed. Drive
          times nobody had driven. Budgets nobody had spent. "Half a day" for
          something that eats a whole one.
        </p>
        <p>
          So I started writing the plans I wanted to read, and the engineering
          half of my brain handles the rest — prices that are checked, timings
          that are real, and entry rules that say when someone last looked.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>Where I've been, and where I haven't</h2>
      <p>
        I've travelled the routes behind the {visited.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and
        {' '}{visited.at(-1)} guides myself. The rest are researched the way I'd
        research my own trip — official sources, current prices, real timings,
        and the same refusal to guess. But I won't imply I've stood somewhere I
        haven't, and when I do get there, the page gets better for it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a small, growing library of day-by-day itineraries.
        Every plan keeps the daily pace realistic, prices the week in real
        numbers, and links the stays and tours it actually recommends so you
        can book them without going hunting.
      </p>
      <p>
        New countries go up as they're researched properly, and nothing goes
        up as a placeholder — which is why the library grows slowly.
      </p>

      <h2>How each itinerary gets built</h2>
    </div>

    <ol class="method">
      {method.map((item, i) => (
        <li class="method__item" data-reveal style={`--stagger:${i}`}>
          <span class="method__num" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h3>{item.step}</h3>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>Ask me about a trip</h2>
      <p>
        Planning is the part I actually enjoy, so if you're going somewhere the
        site doesn't cover yet, ask anyway — I've picked up contacts in a few
        countries along the way, and I'll tell you plainly when I don't know.
        On visas I can help properly for a handful of countries, Russia and
        Japan especially. For everywhere else I'll point you at the authority
        that actually decides, because that's the only answer worth having.
      </p>

      <h2>How it pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        Some of the booking links are affiliate links. If you book through one,
        this site earns a small commission and you pay exactly the same price.
        That's the whole business model — there's no paid placement, and a hotel
        can't buy its way onto a plan.
      </p>

      <h2>One honest caveat</h2>
      <p>
        Entry rules depend on your passport and your residence, and they change
        with very little notice. Everything here is checked when it's written
        and dated on the page, but please confirm with the embassy or your
        airline before you book anything expensive.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="cta-card" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <h2>Planning a trip I haven't covered?</h2>
        <p>Tell me where you're headed — it helps decide what gets researched next.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn--solid" href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>
    </aside>
  </section>
</Base>
